# kk: Compute timestamps now use precise granularity
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46201 state=opened
# ### What does this MR do and why?

<!--Describe in detail what your merge request does and why.-->

Currently, from the 5th consecutive timestamp in a compute encoder, Metal starts returning 0 and KK marks it as available.

So, `MTL4TimestampGranularityRelaxed` changes to `Precise` in `mtl_compute_write_timestamp`.

* Identified in AnyPS5, and it also happens on the M5 Pro (macOS 27.3).
* Minimal test: before, from the 5th timestamp it returned 0; now 0 zeros.
* CTS: 2266 cases, same results, 0 failures.
* Cost: 2000 timestamps go from 132 to 139–146 ms.

Closes: https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/issues/16668

Assisted-by: LLM

Original Language: ESP
--- src/kosmickrisp/bridge/mtl_encoder.m
+++ src/kosmickrisp/bridge/mtl_encoder.m
@@ -504,7 +504,7 @@ mtl_compute_write_timestamp(mtl_compute_encoder *encoder,
       id<MTL4ComputeCommandEncoder> enc =
          (id<MTL4ComputeCommandEncoder>)encoder;
       id<MTL4CounterHeap> h = (id<MTL4CounterHeap>)heap;
-      [enc writeTimestampWithGranularity:MTL4TimestampGranularityRelaxed
+      [enc writeTimestampWithGranularity:MTL4TimestampGranularityPrecise
                                 intoHeap:h
                                  atIndex:index];
    }

